Created: November 18, 2011

Updated: August 6, 2024

Favre Waffle House Fantasy Football League

League Manifesto

PREAMBLE

We, the People of the FWHFFL, do ordain and establish this Constitution for Fantasy Football. The

purpose of this Constitution is to memorialize the great oral tradition of the FWHFFL and to set forth the

rules of the game. This Constitution is a living document, and while it is the intent of the framers of this

Constitution to address herein as many issues as possible that may arise in connection with participation

in the FWHFFL, the framers recognize that this Constitution may not address every issue that may arise.

Accordingly, the People of the FWHFFL pledge to play and participate in this game in accordance with the

rules set forth herein, but more importantly pledge to follow the overriding spirit of this game, which is

to enjoy yourself, to treat all fellow participants with respect and to play with the health of the FWHFFL,

and not just one’s self interest, in mind.

ARTICLE I. OBJECT

To assemble a lineup of 15 players from the National Football League, herein referred to as the NFL,

whose cumulative statistics during the regular season, compiled and measured by the methods

described in these rules, exceed those of all other teams in the League.

ARTICLE II. TEAMS

There are 12 teams participating in a 1 division format consisting of NFL players.

ARTICLE III. ROSTER Construction

A team is composed of a maximum 13 NFL players. No team shall exceed this total for any reason. Any

transaction (e.g., trade, free agent claim) that increases the size of the roster must be accompanied by a

concomitant transaction (e.g, trade, release) that simultaneously returns the roster to its maximum of

13.

A team’s roster must be able to fill an Active lineup at all times.

For players eligible for IR, 1 IR spot is available for designation. When a player is designated for the IR

spot, they can not return to the Active Roster or Reserve Roster until all roster restrictions are met.

Rosters will lock upon the start of Week 14. completion of the following season’s Auction Draft.

No new players may be added via free agency upon

Rosters must adhere to the following limits:

| Roster Spot | Starters | Max Roster |
| --- | --- | --- |
| QB | 1 | 2 |
| RB | 2 | 5 |
| WR | 2 | 8 |
| FLEX | 1 | N/A |
| Limited FLEX | 1 | N/A |

FLEX = WR or RB or TE

Limited FLEX = WR or TE

Bench ROSTER

A team’s reserve roster consists of a maximum of 6 players.

INJURED RESERVE

A player must be eligible for IR to utilize this designation. Only 1 player may occupy IR at any one time.

ARTICLE IV. AUCTION DRAFT

An NFL player auction is conducted on the first weekend after/before the start of the NFL preseason.

Attendance at the Auction Draft (either in person or online) is mandatory for participation in the League.

Failure to participate in the Auction Draft will result in penalties including but not limited to: expulsion

from the League, monetary fines, ridicule, etc.

Each team must acquire 15 players during the Auction Draft. Each team is annually allocated 200

Auction Units (hereinafter referred to at times as “Dollars” or “$”) to acquire these 15 players. (The total

amount of Auction Units that a team has available to acquire its 15 players during the Auction Draft shall

be referred to hereafter as the team’s “Auction Budget.

”)

A team need not spend its entire Auction Budget during the Auction Draft. The order of the draft will be

the previous years reverse final standings order, unless deemed impossible due to technical limitations.

The team bidding first opens with a minimum salary bid of $1 for any eligible player, and the bidding

proceeds around the room at minimum increments of $1 until only one bidder is left. The process is3

repeated, with successive team owners introducing players to be bid on, until every team has a squad of

15 players, by requisite position.

No team may make a bid for a player it cannot afford. For example, a team with $3 left and two openings

on its roster is limited to a maximum bid of $2 for one player.

No team may bid for a player who qualifies only at a position that the team has already filled. For

example, a team that has acquired eight wide receivers may not enter the bidding for any player who

qualifies only at wide receiver. (See Article V regarding position eligibility.)

Players who commence the season on a NFL team’s injury or suspension list are eligible to be drafted.

ARTICLE V. FEES

Annual Dues: $40$100

All fees are to be paid by the conclusion of the Auction Draft. If said fees are not paid by that date, the

roster of the team owing the fees shall be frozen (no roster moves or transactions will be permitted)

until the time that said fees are paid. Acceptable forms of payment include cash and transfers via Google

Wallet or Venmo. No money shall pass directly from team to team under any circumstances. No bets

are to be made on the outcome of any game. All annual dues are payable into the prize pool and are

subsequently distributed to the top 6 teams in the final standings (See Article VII).

ARTICLE VI. PLAYER SALARIES

The salary of a player is determined by the time and means of his acquisition and does not change until a

player is signed to a Guaranteed Long-Term Contract or is released back into the draft pool at the end of

the year.

The salary of a player acquired in the NFL Auction Draft is his auction price.

The salary of a player acquired as an undrafted free agent via the free agent pool is $1.

The salary of a player acquired as a free agent via the free agent pool who was drafted in the Auction

Draft is his auction price.

The salary of a player acquired as a waiver pickup via the waiver system is valued at the highest winning

bid.

ARTICLE VII. PRIZE MONEY

The prize pool shall be divided among the teams qualifying for playoffs. The payout is determined by the

final standings. The payout is as follows:

1st place - - $280

2nd place through 6th place will receive their league entry fee back ($40).4

ARTICLE VIII. STANDINGS

The following criteria are used to determine team performance: Passing, rushing, receiving, and

defensive/special teams stats. (For point breakdowns refer to ARTICLE IX).

Standings will reflect head to head scheduling.

