A decimal scoring system will be used for all categories measuring yards. Rushing and Receiving yards are

scored as 1 yard = 0.15 pts. Passing yards are scored as 1 yard = 0.05 pts.

ARTICLE X. TRADES

Trades may commence between teams from the time the Auction Draft has been completed to the

Wednesday before Thanksgiving. Teams are free to make trades of any kind without limit (except as

stipulated below). There is no limit on the number or type (active or reserve) of players that may be

included in a trade. All of the following structures must be adhered to, however, in the immediate wake

of a trade for such a trade to be valid:

At all times after the trade the active roster of all teams that have participated in the trade must meet

the position requirements established in Article III.

At no point may a team have more than 15 players on its roster.

No trades of any kind may be made between the conclusion of the trade deadline and the completion of

the League Season.

Trades do not affect the salaries or contract status of players.

All trades will be reviewed by the league during a 24 hour wait period. Each team has the opportunity to

“intercept” the trade by negotiating a new trade involving 1 of the traded players with 1 of the agreeing

parties. If the trade is intercepted the 24 hour wait period resets.

ARTICLE XI. MOVEMENT BETWEEN ACTIVE ROSTER AND RESERVE ROSTER

An owner may demote a player from the active roster to the reserve roster, or promote a player in the

reverse direction, at any time and for any reason, such promotions to take effect with the subsequent

transaction deadline. No player, however, may be demoted without being replaced on the active roster

by an eligible player -- that is, a player who fulfills position eligibility requirements (which may include

shifting another active player into the demoted player’s position and the promoted player into the

shifted player’s position)

ARTICLE XII. SIGNING FREE AGENTS

A Free Agent Auction Bidding system is used to make player acquisitions. Each team begins the year

with a budget of $100. All acquisitions will be processed in batches one or more times each week. If a

team would like to add a player, that team will secretly bid a certain amount. On the next waiver process

day, the team with the highest amount bid will be awarded the player, and the bid will be subtracted

from that team’s budget. A team cannot bid more than their current FAAB budget. The minimum bid is

$0.

In the event of bids of equal amounts for a player by two teams (a tie), the team ranked lowest in the

standings is declared the winner (ie. inverse order of standings).6

On days in which an NFL game is scheduled, an Open Free Agent system is in effect. Owners can add and

drop players without waiting for the scheduled free agent bidding period. These transactions will be

fulfilled by the Commissioner For Life.

ARTICLE XIII. GUARANTEED LONG-TERM CONTRACTS / KEEPER RULES

Guaranteed Long-Term Contracts may be awarded to any player with the following exceptions:

A player drafted in the Auction Draft or acquired by free agency may be kept at a price of +$10. The

greater value between the auction draft cost and free agency cost will be used as the base cost for the

keeper contract.

A player who has completed a 1 year GLC may be signed to a 2 year GLC with increases to his salary of

+$15 the first year and +$10 the second year.

(Example: Player X is drafted for $5. A 1 year GLC will cost $15. A 2 year GLC after the 1 year GLC has

been completed will cost $30 for year 1 and $40 for year 2.)

A player who has not completed a 1 year GLC is NOT eligible for a 2 year GLC.

A team may designate any player on its roster as a keeper at the end of a season so long as they can

afford to fill their roster in the following Auction Draft.

Players not signed to a GLC will be eligible for the Auction Draft.

Long-term contracts are entirely transferable, both in rights and obligations; the trade of a player in no

way affects his contract status.

If a player signed to a 2 year GLC is dropped, the team who signed that player to the 2 year GLC is

responsible for that contract against their Auction Budget.

If another team signs a player with a 2 year GLC from the waiver system the signing team is now

obligated to fulfill the terms of that contract.

Once a player has completed a 2 year GLC he re-enters the auction draft.

Exception: Chris Henry rule: A player under a long-term contract that DIES, his contract is considered null

and void.

Travis/Aaron Rodgers Rule: Keepers are designated and locked 1 week prior to the Auction Draft. If an

injury, suspension or arrest occurs to the player during the time frame between when keepers lock and

the draft begins then the owner has the right of first refusal to void the pending keeper contract. This is

only applicable to players signed to new 1 year or 2 year GLC keeper deals.7

Calvin Ridley Clause: a season long suspension does not void a 2 year GLC.

ARTICLE XIV. PLAYOFFS

The top 6 teams will move on to the playoffs starting Week 14.

The 6 seeds will be determined by the following criteria:

1 & 2 seeds are awarded to the top 2 head to head records. playoffs.

Both teams will receive Round 1 byes in the

3, 4 & 5 seeds will be awarded to the teams finishing according to head to head record only.

6 seed will be determined by points scored for. will receive the 6th and final playoff seed.

The remaining team with the highest points scored for

The consolation bracket will be seeded based on head to head record for the remaining 6 teams.

Tie breakers will follow the example below (the example uses a 4 team playoff setting but the same

process will be applied to our 6 team format):

The main things to remember are that teams have to have played the same number of head to head

games amongst each other for that to be used. Remember to start over after each tie is broken.

Team A – 7-5 – 825 points – 3rd in division 1 – beat team B, Lost to team C, lost to team D, beat team E

Team B – 7-5 – 918 points – 2nd in division 1 – lost to team A, beat team C, lost to team E, did not play

team D

Team C – 7-5 – 725 points – 1st in division 2 – beat team A, lost to team B, lost to team D, lost to team E

Team D – 7-5 – 720 points – 2nd in division 2 – beat team A, beat team C, beat team E, did not play team

B

Team E – 7-5 – 920 points 1st in Division 1 – lost to team A, beat team B, beat team C, lost to team D

First we take the top 2 seeds. would get the #1 seed.

Teams E and C won the byes. Since team E beat team C head to head they

We then have to figure out the #3 seed.

Since teams A, B, and D all have 7-5 records we first look at head to head records.8

Team A beat team B and lost to team D, they are 1-1 head to head.

Team B lost to team A, and did not play team D, they are 0-1 head to head.

Team D beat team A, and did not play team B, they are 1-0 head to head.

Since the three teams did not play the same amount of head to head games we have to go to points for.

This Awards the #3 seed to team B as they had 918 points

Now we have to figure out the #4 seed.

Again, teams A and D have identical 7-5 records. This means we look at their head to head matchup.

Since team D beat team A they would get the 4th and final playoff seed.

ARTICLE XV. GOVERNANCE

The FWHFFL is governed by the Commissioner for Life. The Commissioner for Life may designate as many

league participants as from time to time he deems appropriate to participate in certain tasks for the

good of the FWHFFL (such as the drafting of this Constitution). The Commissioner for Life has the

authority to interpret playing rules and to handle all necessary and routine FWHFFL business. The

Commissioner for Life may, at his discretion, or upon motion made by a team owner, also designate as

needed an Executive Committee composed of the Commissioner and two other team owners in good

standing to assist in the interpretation of playing rules. Such an Executive Committee shall make all of its

decisions by majority vote. In the event that the Commissioner for Life is involved in a dispute to be

resolved by the Executive Committee, the Commissioner for Life shall recuse himself from the

deliberations and shall appoint three disinterested team owners instead of two to make the decision. All

decisions, rulings, and interpretations by the Executive Committee are subject to veto by the FWHFFL as

a whole, upon motion for such a veto being promptly made. Any such veto requires a majority vote of all

team owners to pass. In the event of a tie in such a veto vote, the decision of the Executive Committee

shall stand. Rule changes are determined by majority vote of the FWHFFL. Any tie in a vote to change a

rule shall result in the rule remaining unchanged. Rule changes will only be considered during the

off-season, except in exceptional circumstances. Any proposed in-season rule change requires the

approval of three-fourths (3/4) of the FWHFFL as a whole. The Commissioner for Life is limited to

setting lineups, pushing through/killing trades and making free agent pick ups for other teams. The

Commissioner for Life can not make waiver claims for ANY franchise outside of his own.

